A||N||13||Stan86SE||4"X10" Grill Cloth Replacement ||||5||
Z||000000||Stan86SE||05-04-2001||07:40 PM||||I'm replacing the old, faded grill cloth on my 4x10" dash speakers.  What's the best adhesive to use to attach the new cloth to the frames?  3M-type spray adhesive?  Some kind of glue or hot glue?  Any info or tips appreciated, as I'd like to do it right the first time.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Thanks! <P>- Stan<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Stan86SE (edited 05-04-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Ralph||05-04-2001||08:03 PM||||Stan, I purchased a spray can of black Flower dye at JOANNS fabrics. I sprayed the cloth and they look like a million. I did this last summer and the still look fine. The flower dye spray is a flat color and they don't get shiny. Just look new!<P>Ralph<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Stan86SE:<BR><B>I'm replacing the old, faded grill cloth on my 4x10" dash speakers.  What's the best adhesive to use to attach the new cloth to the frames?  3M-type spray adhesive?  Some kind of glue or hot glue?  Any info or tips appreciated, as I'd like to do it right the first time.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Thanks! <P>- Stan<P><BR>[This message has been edited by Stan86SE (edited 05-04-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/yellowformula/LittleFiero.jpg">   <BR>  <B>  Ralph </B>||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||05-04-2001||08:07 PM||||Im gonna get flamed for this, but go to hardware store and get a small can of contact cement, like used for formica counter tops, only cost a couple dollars. brush a light coating around the back edges. lay it upside down on top of material and cut the material leaving an inch or two all around and brush a lite coat on this edge too. let it dry for 5 or 10 minutes, then start by pulling it over and sticking it, working your way all around. It will stick on contact and after a day or two it will be permanent. the spray on glues in a can cant seem to deal with the heat and will come back loose. Use whats left in can for any kind of upolstery work, heat wont affect it.<BR>Just make sure its almost dry before you try to stick it, if its still wet it will come back off. Then just put speaker back in dash. BTW the painging makes them look new if there not torn or saggy. Lisa and I both painted ours black.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 05-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Wolfhound||05-04-2001||09:30 PM||||Radio Shack has a 30" x 36" roll of black grill cloth for a little less than 10 bucks. enough for about 6 cars. I cut the cloth about 3/4 larger than the grill top and lay the grill on top of it. then use spray contact on the excess and edge of the grill. Let it dry and fold over in place and trim. Done! No adhesive on the face of the grill.<BR>Coy||||reg||1
Z||000004||Stan86SE||05-04-2001||09:47 PM||||Thanks a lot for the replies and tips, all!  Before I posted, I had already removed the old cloth (that had faded over time from dark gray to blue).  I've got some new automotive-grade gray grill cloth from Rodney Dickman that looks really close to OEM in weave and color.  Now, with the tips and info that all of you were nice enough to give, I'll soon (hopefully) have nice, new-looking grills!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Thanks again!  This is a GREAT group of people here!<P>- Stan<P>  ||||reg||7
Z||000005||fiero56||05-04-2001||11:39 PM||||I used the spray can stuff on mine. It has stayed very tacky for the 8 months or so, isn't acting like contact cement. The cloth stays in place just fine, and if I ever decide to change the cloth in the future, it will be easy to remove. I used the RadioShack black cabinet speaker cloth for mine. Looks awsome, especially with my dark grey dashboard.||||reg||1
Z||000006||deceler8||05-05-2001||10:42 AM||||Here's a thought...ditch the cloth altogether.<P>I'm running the grilles without the cloth and they look great.<P>You could also paint the bare grilles the color of your dash or use a contrasting color. Since they're plastic, any spray paint will work.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Stan86SE||05-05-2001||10:59 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by deceler8:<BR><B>Here's a thought...ditch the cloth altogether.<P>I'm running the grilles without the cloth and they look great.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>deceler8, you know, after I took off the old cloth and saw the color of the plastic grilles and that they were in exc shape, that thought of yours actually occurred to me, too!  Not a bad idea at all...  (Great minds must think alike!)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">   But, since I already bouoht the new cloth and it looks good, too, I'll probably end up using it.<P>- Stan ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Bruce||05-05-2001||03:32 PM||||I took mine to an upholstery shop and had it done for free. Of course, I had my driver's side seat recovered there, so they were nice to me. It saved me a lot of headachces, since I probably would have glued my fingers to the frames!||||reg||1
Z||000009||Cheever2||05-10-2001||04:38 PM||||I just finished recovering mine with Radio Shack Grill Fabric and a hot glue gun.  Quick & easy.  Can't put em in the car until it comes back from the shop.  Now what should I do with all this leftover fabric?<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000010||Stan86SE||05-10-2001||05:12 PM||||I ended up using the gray OEM-style fabric from Rodney Dickman.  I put it on using the spray-on 3M Super 77 adhesive.  It looks really good and I'm very happy with the results.  It was simple to do and hopefully the Super 77 glue will stand up to the heat over time - (I'm taking your warning about that seriously, rogergarrison).  Time will tell...<P>- Stan||||reg||1
Z||000011||Nashco||05-10-2001||07:32 PM||||I realized that the underside of the cloth has never been hit by the sun the other day when I pulled mine off.  I next made the realization that the speaker covers are exactly symmetrical.  So, my conclusion was that the speaker cover fabric could be peeled off, flipped over, and glued onto the other speaker cover.  I just did this, coincidentally, and took pictures of everything.  I'll make a post tonight with pictures and my process.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000012||rodmcneill||05-11-2001||12:09 AM||||Now, that's what I call recycling! Good idea.||||reg||1
Z||000013||sjp777||05-11-2001||12:25 AM||||I removed the cloth and used a dremel cutting wheel to cut out the plastic grill covering the oval speaker opening. Don't need that blocking the highs. I recovered the frames first with a very sheer black fabric (almost like black panty hose), then over that with black fiberglass window screening from home depot. Used hot glue to attach both layers on the underside of the frame. Finished product looks awesome with the dark grey of the upper dash, the window screen material has a sheen to it that matches perfectly.<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
